  "title": "Count and Say",
  "summary": "The count-and-say sequence is defined recursively.",
  "statement": "The count-and-say sequence is defined recursively. The first term is '1'. Each subsequent term is the run-length encoding of the previous term. Run-length encoding means expressing consecutive duplicates of characters as a count followed by the character. For example, the sequence's second term is '11' (one '1'), third term is '21' (two '1's), fourth term is '1211' (one '2', and one '1'), and so on. Given a positive integer n, provide the n-th term of this sequence.",
  "constraints": [
    "1 <= n <= 30.",
    "Term 1 is the string '1'. Each later term describes maximal consecutive equal-digit runs in the previous term as count followed by digit.",
    "Return a string. Encode consecutive runs separately, not the total frequency of each digit."
  ],
